<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="ClaudeBuddy.OrbWindow"
        Title="Claude Buddy"
        Width="56" Height="56"
        SizeToContent="Manual"
        WindowDecorations="None"
        TransparencyLevelHint="Transparent"
        Background="Transparent"
        Topmost="True"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        CanResize="False"
        ShowActivated="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual">

    <!-- Root is pinned to 56x56 and anchored top-left rather than left to
         fill the window, so the orb's geometry does not depend on the window
         being the size it asked for. Windows enforces a minimum size on a
         top-level window, and a window below it is inflated rather than
         refused; its *position* is still set exactly, so pinning the real
         56x56 box to the top-left corner keeps a 36px circle centred at DIP
         (28,28) — the point the mic flyout (OrbWindow.OrbCentre) and the team
         arrows (TeamLinks, PointToScreen of the orb's CentreDip) anchor on —
         wherever the window is bigger than its content.

         This comment used to say the floor was SM_CXMINTRACK/SM_CYMINTRACK
         (136x39) and that it landed as a 120x56 client area, even for a
         window asking for 26x26. That was wrong on the axis and on the size.
         Measured 2026-09-26 for CB-198 on a Windows 11 box at 96 DPI
         (SM_CXMINTRACK=136, SM_CYMINTRACK=39, which the window does not
         follow), with this very class: OrbWindow hosted by a harness that
         skips Program.Main's single-instance claim, its Win32 style 0x16020000
         and ex-style 0x200008 identical to the installed 0.5.7-beta's orbs.
         Asked 56 → 56x56. Asked 33.6 → 33x39. Asked 36.4 → 36x39. Asked 26 →
         32x39. So the floor is about 32 wide by 39 tall, and at the default
         size neither applies: the window is exactly 56x56, as the installed
         app's own orbs also read. Every figure is a window and client
         rectangle, not a picture of the drawn circle — GetWindowRect and
         GetClientRect on the window handle (EnumWindows by pid, via
         PowerShell Add-Type P/Invoke, for the installed app), agreeing with
         Avalonia's ClientSize — run as an interactive scheduled task in the
         console session, since an ssh session is session 0 and cannot see
         the desktop's windows at all.

         The 39-DIP height is the floor that matters to an orb. The strip it
         adds below a smaller one is not click-through — it swallows clicks,
         right-clicks and hover rather than passing them to the window
         behind — which is why OrbSizing.MinWindows is 0.7, the smallest size
         whose window clears both floors; its comment has that measurement.

         Transparent (not null) background so the whole 56x56 area is
         hit-testable: hover tooltip, drag, and right-click work anywhere
         on the orb's square, not just the ellipse pixels.

         CB-198: the user's orb size is a LayoutTransform over Root, not a
         change to anything inside it. Root keeps its 56x56 geometry — circle,
         glow, context ring, badges and SetTeamRole's 0.72 all mean exactly
         what they did — and the window is sized to WindowDip(OrbSize) around
         it; see OrbWindow.ApplyOrbSize. The transform control is anchored
         Left/Top for the same floor reason Root is. -->
    <LayoutTransformControl x:Name="SizeTransform"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top">
        <LayoutTransformControl.LayoutTransform>
            <ScaleTransform ScaleX="1" ScaleY="1"/>
        </LayoutTransformControl.LayoutTransform>
    <Grid x:Name="Root" Background="Transparent"
          Width="56" Height="56"
          HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top">
        <Grid.ContextMenu>
            <!-- Opening is where "End this session" finds out what is running
                 underneath this session's pid. Not in UpdateFrom, which runs
                 for every orb every couple of seconds: the answer costs a `ps`
                 of the whole process table, and the question only matters at
                 the instant somebody is aiming at the row. See
                 OrbWindow.SessionMenu_Opening and SessionDependents. -->
            <ContextMenu Opening="SessionMenu_Opening" Closed="SessionMenu_Closed">
                <!-- Chat name, then its directory. The path row hides itself
                     when there's no name yet, since it'd just repeat. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="SessionInfoItem" IsEnabled="False"/>
                <MenuItem x:Name="SessionPathItem" IsEnabled="False"/>
                <!-- CB-168: opens NewChatWindow pre-filled with this orb's
                     cwd and CLI. Shown only for a local-CLI orb (IsLocalCli)
                     — an OpenClaw orb pre-fills its agent instead, on the
                     slot ines-carvalho's OpenClaw picker fills in; visibility
                     for both is set in UpdateFrom, next to the other
                     session-dependent rows. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="NewChatHereItem"
                          Header="New chat here"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          Click="NewChatHere_Click"/>
                <Separator/>
                <!-- Only shown for an orb the user has dragged; see
                     OrbWindow.SetPinned. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="ResetPositionItem"
                          Header="Reset this orb's position"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          Click="ResetPosition_Click"/>
                <!-- Local hooks own their status files, so their displayed
                     state can be reset here. OpenClaw and remote-control
                     sessions instead receive a disabled explanation from
                     UpdateFrom: their owner is the only thing that can change
                     their state. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="ResetIdleItem"
                          Header="Reset this session to idle"
                          ToolTip.Tip="Changes this orb's displayed state only; the next hook event may update it again."
                          Click="ResetIdle_Click"/>
                <!-- CB-167's per-orb sound override. Both submenus start
                     empty and are (re)built in SessionMenu_Opening rather
                     than here or in UpdateFrom — listing the system sound
                     drawer means reading the disk, and Opening is the one
                     moment that actually costs something, same reasoning as
                     ApplyEndSessionGuard just above. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="SoundMenuItem" Header="Sound">
                    <MenuItem x:Name="SoundFinishedMenuItem" Header="When a turn finishes"/>
                    <MenuItem x:Name="SoundAttentionMenuItem" Header="When it needs you"/>
                </MenuItem>
                <!-- CB-198's per-orb size: Default (follow the slider) plus
                     OrbSizing.Presets, rebuilt on Opening beside the Sound
                     submenus for the same reason — see
                     OrbWindow.RebuildSizeSubmenu. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="SizeMenuItem" Header="Size"/>
                <!-- The `claude agents` roster, for the orbs it lists. Above
                     the two lifecycle items because it is the harmless one of
                     the three, and hidden rather than greyed for the same
                     reason they are — see ClickRouting.OffersTheAgentsView,
                     which is also where the reasoning lives for why this is a
                     menu item and not what a double-click does. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="AgentsViewItem"
                          Header="Open agents view"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          Click="AgentsView_Click"/>
                <!-- CB-170: the OpenClaw conversation's own two verbs, which
                     are requests to the gateway rather than anything done to a
                     process here. Hidden unless the gateway will take them —
                     SessionPresence.CanInterruptOpenClaw /
                     CanEndOpenClawConversation, set in UpdateFrom.

                     StaysOpenOnClick because the answer is written back onto
                     the row: "End the conversation" arms on the first click
                     and acts on the second, and either row then says what the
                     gateway said. A menu that closed on the click would take
                     the refusal with it. Header, tooltip and IsEnabled are all
                     set in code; see OrbWindow.RestoreOpenClawRows. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="InterruptRunItem"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          StaysOpenOnClick="True"
                          Click="InterruptRun_Click"/>
                <MenuItem x:Name="EndConversationItem"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          StaysOpenOnClick="True"
                          Click="EndConversation_Click"/>
                <!-- The two lifecycle actions, hidden for a session they cannot
                     serve rather than greyed out — see
                     SessionPresence.CanDismiss/CanEndSession, which is also
                     where the reasoning lives. Visibility is set in
                     OrbWindow.UpdateFrom.

                     Neither asks for confirmation, and that is a decision
                     rather than an omission: this app has no dialog vocabulary
                     anywhere, and a context menu is already a two-step gesture
                     (right-click, then aim). Dismiss is undone by the session's
                     next hook event; End is the one that is not, which is why
                     it is worded as what it does to the session rather than to
                     the orb. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="DismissItem"
                          Header="Dismiss this orb"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          Click="Dismiss_Click"/>
                <!-- Header, tooltip and IsEnabled are all rewritten on Opening
                     for a session that turns out to be hosting the background
                     daemon — CB-26. Disabled with an explanation rather than
                     hidden, which is the opposite call from the one above it:
                     hiding is right when the answer to "why not" is "this
                     conversation lives somewhere else", and wrong here, where
                     the answer is "this orb looks finished and is actually your
                     window onto running work" — which is precisely the thing
                     the user does not know and the reason they were about to
                     click. -->
                <MenuItem x:Name="EndSessionItem"
                          Header="End this session"
                          IsVisible="False"
                          Click="EndSession_Click"/>
                <!-- Exit is the whole app, not this session — worth a rule
                     between it and two items that end one thing each. -->
                <Separator/>
                <MenuItem Header="Exit Claude Buddy" Click="Exit_Click"/>
            </ContextMenu>
        </Grid.ContextMenu>

        <!-- Soft glow behind the orb (fill assigned in code-behind, shared
             with the orb so color fades hit both) -->
        <!-- Soft glow behind the orb. A RadialGradientBrush, not a BlurEffect:
             the orb animates continuously, and a blur next to it was re-blurred
             every frame at ~7% of a core per orb. Fill is assigned in
             code-behind so colour changes reach it. -->
        <Ellipse x:Name="Glow" Width="56" Height="56"/>

        <!-- How full this session's context window is, for the sessions whose
             roster says. Outside the orb rather than inside it, because the
             orb's own three channels are already spoken for — its fill is the
             state, its ring is the identity, its glow is the state again — and
             a fourth drawn on top of any of them would be a fourth meaning
             competing for the same pixels.

             A Canvas rather than shapes dropped straight into Root: the arc's
             geometry is in absolute coordinates about the orb's centre at
             (28,28), and a Path laid out by a Grid is measured to its own
             bounds and then centred, which silently moves the arc somewhere
             else depending on how much of the circle it happens to sweep. The
             account orbs are a Canvas for the same reason.

             Hidden entirely until something reports a percentage. Nobody
             saying is not the same as zero, and a full track drawn around a
             session with no reading claims a measurement that was never taken.
             The arithmetic is UsageRingGeometry's — the same one the account
             orbs use, not a second copy of the trig. -->
        <Canvas x:Name="ContextRingLayer"
                Width="56" Height="56"
                IsVisible="False"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <Ellipse x:Name="ContextTrack"
                     Canvas.Left="6" Canvas.Top="6"
                     Width="44" Height="44"
                     Stroke="#17FFFFFF" StrokeThickness="3"/>
            <Path x:Name="ContextArc" StrokeThickness="3" StrokeLineCap="Round"/>
        </Canvas>

        <!-- An agent's picture, beneath the Orb ellipse so that ellipse's ring
             still draws on top of it. Its own Image rather than an ImageBrush
             on the ellipse's fill (which it was until CB-198): Avalonia's Skia
             backend rasterises a tile brush into an intermediate surface sized
             to its target in DIPs at the screen's DPI, and never counts the
             render transform above it — so under the orb-size LayoutTransform
             a 2x orb drew a 36-pixel picture stretched over 72, a visible
             mosaic on the first real Windows install. An Image is drawn
             through the whole transform with no intermediate, and stays sharp
             at every size. Width, Height and the clip follow SetTeamRole;
             the pulse shares the Orb's transform. -->
        <Image x:Name="AvatarImage"
               Width="36" Height="36"
               Stretch="UniformToFill"
               IsVisible="False"
               IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <Image.Clip>
                <EllipseGeometry Rect="0,0,36,36"/>
            </Image.Clip>
        </Image>

        <!-- The buddy itself -->
        <Ellipse x:Name="Orb"
                 Width="36" Height="36"
                 Stroke="#22FFFFFF"
                 StrokeThickness="1"/>

        <!-- What kind of conversation this is, for gateway sessions: a
             scheduled job, a private message, or a room with other people in
             it. Bottom-right, the way a presence dot rides an avatar, because
             the orb's other three channels are already spoken for — the fill is
             the face or the letter, the ring is the agent, and the glow is the
             state.

             Its own opaque fill rather than a bare glyph: this sits on top of a
             photograph as often as not, and a glyph alone disappears into a
             light patch of one. Hidden unless the kind is known, so a local
             session and an agent's own main session carry nothing — a mark on
             almost every orb distinguishes nothing. -->
        <Border x:Name="KindBadge"
                Width="22" Height="22"
                IsVisible="False"
                CornerRadius="11"
                Background="#F2202024"
                BorderBrush="#59FFFFFF" BorderThickness="1"
                HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                Margin="0,0,4,4"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <!-- Two ways to wear a kind, and a kind picks one. @ and # are
                 punctuation the user already reads as the thing, every font
                 has them and no emoji face is ever involved, so a TextBlock is
                 right and costs nothing. Every other kind is drawn: the clock,
                 gear, arrows and cloud all resolve through font fallback to
                 Segoe UI Emoji on Windows, a colour font, so as text they
                 arrive in that font's own colours and size and ignore
                 Foreground entirely (CB-171, CB-173). A mark this small has to
                 be designed at this size, not borrowed from a font at it. See
                 KindMarkFor and SymbolMarks.

                 Both live in a Panel because a Border takes one child and
                 exactly one of the two is ever visible. -->
            <Panel>
                <TextBlock x:Name="KindGlyph"
                           FontSize="13"
                           Foreground="#F2FFFFFF"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                <Path x:Name="KindMark"
                      Width="13" Height="13"
                      IsVisible="False"
                      Stretch="Uniform"
                      Fill="#F2FFFFFF"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
            </Panel>
        </Border>

        <!-- A beating heart, for a session the gateway's heartbeat drives.
             See OpenClawHeartbeat for which those are and how they're
             recognised.

             Top-right rather than sharing the bottom-right slot with KindBadge,
             because the two answer different questions and a session can want
             both: a heartbeat retargeted at a channel is still a channel. The
             orb's other four channels are spoken for (fill, ring, glow, badge),
             and this is the corner that was left.

             It pulses because that is the whole content of the signal — the
             thing on the other end is a timer, and a still heart on a quiet orb
             says only "scheduled", which the clock badge already says better.
             The motion is what distinguishes "something wakes this up" from
             "somebody is waiting for you", which is the confusion the badge
             exists to clear up. Driven from the orb's own shared pulse ticker
             rather than an Avalonia animation — see StartPulse for why that
             matters at one window per orb. -->
        <Border x:Name="HeartBadge"
                Width="22" Height="22"
                IsVisible="False"
                CornerRadius="11"
                Background="#F2202024"
                BorderBrush="#59FFFFFF" BorderThickness="1"
                HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="0,4,4,0"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <!-- Drawn, not typed (SymbolMarks.Heart, set in the constructor):
                 Segoe UI's U+2665 is a third smaller than the macOS one at the
                 same point size, so the same badge wore two hearts (CB-173). -->
            <Path x:Name="HeartGlyph"
                  Width="11" Height="11"
                  Stretch="Uniform"
                  Fill="#FFFF6B81"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
        </Border>

        <!-- Presence: what this session wants from you, when the answer is
             "something" or "nothing ever again".

             Top-*left*, which is the corner the other three channels left. The
             kind badge is bottom-right and the heart is top-right, and both are
             about what a session *is*; this is about whether anything is on the
             other end of it, and a session can want both said at once — a
             background job (gear, bottom-right) that is holding a question (mark,
             top-left).

             One border with the glyph swapped rather than two badges: the two
             answers are mutually exclusive by construction (a job cannot be
             holding a question and finished at the same time), so two controls
             would be two things to keep from colliding for no case that exists.

             Same opaque fill as the others, and for the same reason: this sits on
             top of whatever the orb's fill is, including a photograph. -->
        <Border x:Name="PresenceBadge"
                Width="22" Height="22"
                IsVisible="False"
                CornerRadius="11"
                Background="#F2202024"
                BorderBrush="#59FFFFFF" BorderThickness="1"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="4,4,0,0"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <TextBlock x:Name="PresenceGlyph"
                       FontSize="13"
                       Foreground="#F2FFFFFF"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
        </Border>

        <!-- Which CLI this session is. Bottom-left, the corner the other three
             badges left. Same 22px disc as kind, heart and presence so a
             lobster next to an @ does not make the @ look like a speck.

             Hidden for remote-control orbs, which are Claude Code on another
             machine and already wear a kind badge. OpenClaw keeps the lobster
             even when it also has a kind badge — those two answer different
             questions. -->
        <Border x:Name="CliBadge"
                Width="22" Height="22"
                IsVisible="False"
                CornerRadius="11"
                BorderBrush="#73FFFFFF" BorderThickness="1"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                Margin="4,0,0,4"
                IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <Path x:Name="CliGlyph"
                  Width="14" Height="14"
                  Stretch="Uniform"
                  Fill="#F2FFFFFF"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
        </Border>

        <!-- Folder initial so you can tell orbs apart at a glance
             (falls back to a dot until the session reports its cwd) -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="Glyph"
                   Text="&#8226;"
                   FontSize="16"
                   FontWeight="Bold"
                   Foreground="#DDFFFFFF"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
    </Grid>
    </LayoutTransformControl>
</Window>
